DELUSER(8)                  System Manager's Manual                 DELUSER(8)

NAME
       deluser, delgroup - remove a user or group from the system

SYNOPSIS
       deluser [options] USER
       deluser --system [options] USER
       deluser --group GROUP
       delgroup [--system] [--only-if-empty] GROUP
       deluser USER GROUP

DESCRIPTION
       deluser and delgroup remove users and groups from the system.  They are
       the counterpart to adduser(8) and operate on the account databases
       /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow and /etc/group.  The program behaves as
       delgroup when it is invoked under that name.

       Five modes of operation are available:

       deluser USER
              Remove a normal user.  The user is removed from /etc/passwd,
              /etc/shadow and the member list of every group.  If the user had
              a private group of the same name that is now unused, it is
              removed as well.  The home directory is kept unless --remove-home
              or --remove-all-files is given.

       deluser --system USER
              Remove USER only if it is a system user (uid 100..999).  If the
              user does not exist, no error is returned.

       deluser --group GROUP, delgroup GROUP
              Remove a group.  The group must not be the primary group of any
              user.

       delgroup --system GROUP
              Remove GROUP only if it is a system group (gid 100..999).

       deluser USER GROUP
              Remove USER from GROUP.  A user cannot be removed from their
              primary group.

       Only the superuser may remove users or groups.

OPTIONS
       --system              only remove a system user or group; a missing
                             account is not an error
       --group               remove a group (default when run as delgroup)
       --remove-home         remove the user's home directory and mailspool
       --remove-all-files    remove every file on the system owned by the user
                             (implies --remove-home)
       --backup              archive the home directory to USER.tar before the
                             files are removed (uncompressed tar)
       --backup-to DIR       write the backup archive into DIR (implies
                             --backup)
       --backup-suffix STR   accepted for compatibility; archives are
                             uncompressed tar regardless
       --only-if-empty       (group modes) only remove the group if it has no
                             members
       --quiet, --verbose, --debug
                             adjust the amount of output
       --conf FILE           configuration file (accepted, unused)
       --help                display this help
       --version             display version

FILES
       /etc/passwd   user account database
       /etc/shadow   encrypted passwords
       /etc/group    group database

SECURITY
       deluser needs root privileges.  Do not grant partial privileges to
       deluser with restricted arguments; that is easy to circumvent.

SEE ALSO
       adduser(8), addgroup(8), passwd(1), groups(1)

